WEBVTT

00:04.662 --> 00:07.275
This is Allen Company first-aid training on broken

00:07.275 --> 00:10.300
bones and splinting. Before you help anyone,

00:10.300 --> 00:12.275
make sure the scene is safe for you.

00:12.275 --> 00:15.050
If equipment is involved, get it stopped and locked

00:15.050 --> 00:19.113
out. Then call for help: 911 and the mine office,

00:19.113 --> 00:21.225
so someone meets the ambulance at the gate and

00:21.225 --> 00:22.250
guides it in.

00:23.377 --> 00:25.314
This is first-aid awareness training.

00:25.314 --> 00:28.802
It follows first-aid methods acceptable to MSHA,

00:28.802 --> 00:31.352
based on the American Heart Association first-aid

00:31.352 --> 00:34.214
guidelines that Allen uses for hands-on training.

00:34.214 --> 00:37.177
It supports, but does not replace, your hands-on

00:37.177 --> 00:39.539
first-aid practice with the safety manager,

00:39.539 --> 00:41.514
the same day as this course.

00:42.630 --> 00:45.668
If the person fell, was struck, or was in a vehicle

00:45.668 --> 00:48.718
accident, assume a neck or back injury.

00:48.718 --> 00:51.468
Don't move them unless they're in immediate danger.

00:51.468 --> 00:53.668
Hold the head still in line with the body,

00:53.668 --> 00:56.668
and keep them calm and warm until help arrives.

01:00.005 --> 01:02.917
Signs of a fracture: pain, swelling,

01:02.917 --> 01:06.079
bruising, a limb that looks bent or shortened,

01:06.079 --> 01:08.555
and not being able to use it. The person may have

01:08.555 --> 01:11.755
heard a snap. In an open fracture, the bone breaks

01:11.755 --> 01:14.892
the skin. Treat any injury that might be a fracture

01:14.892 --> 01:15.705
as one.

01:19.084 --> 01:22.034
For an open fracture, control bleeding with pressure

01:22.034 --> 01:24.959
around the wound, not on the bone. Cover it with

01:24.959 --> 01:27.809
a sterile dressing. Don't push the bone back in.

01:28.953 --> 01:31.303
Splint only if you've been trained and help is

01:31.303 --> 01:34.115
far away or the person must be moved.

01:34.115 --> 01:36.515
Splint it the way you found it; don't try to straighten

01:36.515 --> 01:39.228
it. The splint should cover the joints above and

01:39.228 --> 01:42.478
below the break. Pad it. Check circulation below

01:42.478 --> 01:45.752
the injury before and after: skin color,

01:45.752 --> 01:49.403
warmth and feeling. If fingers or toes turn pale,

01:49.403 --> 01:52.140
blue or numb, loosen the ties.

01:53.270 --> 01:56.433
A rigid splint can be a board, or a padded moldable

01:56.433 --> 01:59.608
splint from the first-aid kit. A sling and a swathe

01:59.608 --> 02:02.657
hold an arm against the chest. For a leg with nothing

02:02.657 --> 02:05.595
else available, pad between the legs and tie the

02:05.595 --> 02:07.458
injured leg to the good one.

02:08.576 --> 02:10.964
Treat a dislocation like a fracture:

02:10.964 --> 02:13.651
splint it as found, and never try to pop it back

02:13.651 --> 02:17.026
in. For sprains, rest, a cold pack wrapped in a

02:17.026 --> 02:18.913
cloth, and get it checked.

02:20.055 --> 02:23.230
Big fractures, especially the thigh or pelvis,

02:23.230 --> 02:26.192
can cause shock. Keep the person lying down,

02:26.192 --> 02:28.730
cover them with a blanket, and don't give food

02:28.730 --> 02:31.992
or drink. Every mine keeps first-aid materials,

02:31.992 --> 02:34.392
stretchers and blankets ready for this.

02:37.716 --> 02:40.953
Recap: scene safe, call for help, don't move a

02:40.953 --> 02:44.066
possible spine injury. Splint as found,

02:44.066 --> 02:47.841
joints above and below, padded, check circulation.

02:47.841 --> 02:50.641
Cover open fractures. Treat for shock.

02:51.774 --> 02:54.936
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

02:54.936 --> 02:56.436
override this video.
